// The Appeal, ch9: Test Day. Tuesday, June 1 to Saturday, June 12, 2027 (Federal on Tuesday; Argumentative Writing at the kitchen table Wednesday; two Wednesday calls; Federal on Thursday; the kitchen table Friday night; Saturday in Teo's truck, the Prometric center and its parking lot).
// Coverage per the bible: scenes LR04 LR06 LR10 LR18 LR03 LR09 RC01 RC02 RC07 RC08 RC09 RC04; quiz LR04 LR06x2 LR10 LR18x2 LR03 LR09 LR01 LR08 LR12 LR02 RC01x2 RC02 RC07 RC08 RC09.
// Full-length passage (p2): the first instinct fallacy, Kruger, Wirtz and Miller, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology 88(5), 2005, 725 to 735, read 2026-10-17 from the PDF: Study 1, 1,561 students, Fall 2000 PSYCH 100 midterm, University of Illinois, eraser marks; 3,291 changed answers, 1,690 (51 percent) wrong to right, 838 (25 percent) right to wrong, 763 (23 percent) wrong to wrong; of 1,231 changers, 54 percent helped, 19 percent hurt; 51 randomly chosen students predicted right to wrong would outnumber wrong to right; surveys from 1929 on, about three in four students believe changing lowers scores; Study 2 anticipated regret; Study 3 recall weeks later; the grocery line comparison. Written fresh. Short case passage (p1): the 2007 autopsy (new, fiction).
// Test day facts read 2026-10-17 on lsac.org: LSATdates (June 9 to 12, 2027; Argumentative Writing opens June 1; score release June 30); About LSAT Argumentative Writing and Getting Ready (50 minutes, 15 prewriting with the option to move on after 5, 35 writing; a debatable issue with three or four perspectives; take a position and address some of the others; unscored but required before a score is released; opens eight days before the administration; remote, webcam, photo ID, 360 degree room scan, enclosed room with no glass walls or doors, only ID, water in a clear container with the label removed and foam earplugs on the desk, no pens, pencils or paper, a digital notes box; processing can take up to three weeks); In-Person Checklist, Getting Ready for Your LSAT, Dos and Don'ts, About the 10-Minute Intermission (Prometric test centers; arrive up to 30 minutes early; government photo ID; LawHub username and password; lockers for phones, food and drink; certifying statement; earmuffs assigned at the center; four 35 minute sections, intermission after section two, up to five hours; tell the proctor, no devices, no talk about the test, no scratch paper, section three won't begin until the full ten minutes are up). A Prometric center in the Denver Tech Center area (business listings, 2026-10-17; Prometric's own locator not confirmed); no address on the page.
// Weekdays computed 2026-10-17: Tuesday, June 1; Wednesday, June 2; Wednesday, June 9; Thursday, June 10; Friday, June 11; Saturday, June 12, 2027; Thursday, June 17, 2010 (and June 17, 2027 is a Thursday, the seventeenth anniversary). The 2010 opinion props match ch1 and ch6: eleven pages, nine contentions, the jury instruction, the juror who may have been asleep and the hearsay ruling among the first eight, the first eight over ten pages, the identification sufficiency claim one sentence on page eleven in a paragraph that opens on remaining contentions.
// Reveal schedule: Teo confirms he was in the truck and that he hit the man (f9s12 and closing). The autopsy is planted (f9s8, p1): the brow laceration is superficial and non-contributory; nobody connects it to anything. Kept off the page per the bible's calls: the truck's departure minute, the grandmother refrain, the flashlight's end, Teo on the basement stairs, the gas station. Luis is never called smaller.
// Flags set here: crammed (f9s8). Read here: crammed (f9s9, f9s10, closing), pushedteo (f9s9, f9s12, closing), wroteshannon (f9s8), overclaimed (f9s7).
// All questions, passages and answer choices are original. Not affiliated with or endorsed by LSAC.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 9, part: `Part Three: The Truth`, title: `Test Day`, weeks: `June 2027`,
  tasks: [`LR01`,`LR02`,`LR03`,`LR04`,`LR06`,`LR08`,`LR09`,`LR10`,`LR12`,`LR18`,`RC01`,`RC02`,`RC04`,`RC07`,`RC08`,`RC09`],
  cast: [
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 30. Diesel mechanic at a bus garage. Said yeah in four seconds.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard. Lost Luis's appeal in 2010 and still keeps the opinion in her top drawer.`],
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, 62, at Sterling. Calls Wednesdays at seven.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Tuesday, June 1. The writing part of the test opened that morning, eleven days ahead of the rest of it, and Dani knew because it had been on the kitchen calendar since December in pencil with a box drawn around it. LSAT Argumentative Writing. Fifty minutes at home on her own laptop, with a camera watching her the whole time. No score. The law schools would read it anyway.`,
    `Since that yeah, her brother had sent her exactly one more text, the Thursday after: what time. She'd told him. He'd sent back a thumbs up, which from Teo was close to a paragraph.`,
    `On the 6:52 she did one question a stop and missed two of eleven, both the same kind, and typed them into the log on her phone, like every miss since October. The log was long now. She scrolled to the top of it once, to the first week, and then put the phone away.`,
    `Ana's Tuesday was short. Nothing on the desk but a kitchen timer, a stack of index cards and one last practice passage, about eraser marks, that she'd made Dani read cold at the door. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"No case,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not tonight. Thursday, the opinion. Saturday, not a word of it. Tonight's the test. Sit."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: test day`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Four sections, one break`,
        body: [`June on Federal. The laundromat has a box fan in the door and a man asleep in a plastic chair next to it, and Dicta's asleep on my rug with her feet going. Pencil. Tonight is about the morning itself, because people lose points there that they'd already earned.`,
          `Here's the test. Four sections, thirty five minutes each. Two are scored Logical Reasoning, one is scored Reading Comprehension, and one is an extra section that doesn't count, which can be either kind and can sit anywhere in the order. Nobody will tell you which one it is, and how hard a section felt won't tell you either. So as far as you're concerned every section counts, first through last. There are no logic games anymore. They went away in August 2024, and what took their place is more of what you've done all year.`,
          `After section two you get ten minutes. The test is still running. Your phone stays in the locker, you don't talk to a soul about a single question, you don't touch your scratch paper, and you're back in your chair before the clock runs out. Section three won't start early because you came back early, so use the time. Water, the bathroom, a walk down the hall.`,
          `Every question is worth the same, and a wrong answer costs you nothing a blank wouldn't. Read that twice. A blank is the only answer that's sure to be wrong. When one question is eating your clock, it's worth exactly what the easy one three down is worth, so give it its share of the time, put in your best guess, flag it, and go get the easy one. Come back if there's time. With a minute left and three you haven't opened, pick a letter and fill all three, then spend whatever seconds are left reading one of them.`],
        terms: [[`Unscored section`,`One extra section, Logical Reasoning or Reading Comprehension, that doesn't count toward your score. It can come anywhere in the order, and you can't tell which one it is.`],[`Intermission`,`The ten minute break after section two. The test is still in session: no phone, no talking about the test, no scratch paper.`]],
        exam: `You can get to the test center up to thirty minutes early with a valid government photo ID and your LawHub username and password, memorized or on paper, since your phone goes in a locker. LSAC's test day checklist and its dos and don'ts page spell it all out. Read them the week of your test.` },
      { h: `Both of them true`,
        body: [`One kind of question shows up on nearly every test and asks you to explain something that looks impossible. Two facts, both true, that seem to fight. A ski hill had its snowiest winter in ten years and sold fewer lift tickets than the winter before. The stem asks what most helps resolve the discrepancy, or explain it.`,
          `Don't pick a side. The question already told you both facts are true, so an answer that says one of them is wrong is wrong. You want the fact that makes both of them sensible at once. Say the highway over the pass was closed by snow on half the weekends that winter. Now the snow and the empty lifts fit together. The same snow did both.`,
          `The traps explain one fact and leave the other exactly as strange as it was. Lift tickets cost more this year? That's a reason for fewer tickets, and it says nothing about the snow. What you want goes through both facts, and usually it's a difference nobody mentioned: a different group of people, a different stretch of time, something counted two different ways.`],
        terms: [[`Paradox`,`Two facts that both hold but seem to conflict. The right answer shows how both can be true.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which answer most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy, explain the paradox, or reconcile the findings. Check your choice against both facts. If it only touches one, it isn't the answer.` },
      { h: `Old friends`,
        body: [`Everything else you'll see Saturday you already know. Here it is in one place, so you hear it all together once.`,
          `Must be true is the strictest question on the test. The answer has to follow from what's given, every time, with nothing added. Most strongly supported is a step looser: the passage has to back the answer better than any other, even if it doesn't guarantee it. On both, the trap is the answer that goes one word too far. Some becomes most. Can becomes will. The right answer usually sounds smaller than you want it to.`,
          `A necessary assumption is something an argument can't do without. Negate the choice, and if the argument falls apart, you've got it. A flaw question asks you to say what went wrong in plain words before you look at the choices, and the usual ones haven't changed since December. A trait that fits many people treated as if it fits one. A sample that picked itself. A cause read into two things that happen together. A requirement treated as a guarantee.`,
          `And the blank at the end of an argument, the one that says so and then leaves you a line. Fill it with what the premises support. Not the most exciting thing they could support. Read it back with therefore in front of it, the way we did in October, and listen to whether it holds.`],
        terms: [[`Must be true`,`An answer that follows from the passage with no added assumptions.`],[`Most strongly supported`,`An answer the passage backs better than any other choice, even though it may not be guaranteed.`]],
        exam: `Stems for these say must be true, most strongly supported, the argument depends on assuming, most vulnerable to criticism, and most logically completes the argument. Saturday you won't have time to name every flaw. You'll have time to ask what the argument needed and didn't have.` },
      { h: `Reading a court`,
        body: [`A court opinion is a passage with a job to do. Somebody appealed. The court says what they argued and what it decided, then why, one issue at a time. The main point is nearly always the decision itself, affirmed or reversed. Don't confuse one reason the court gives along the way with the point. One issue isn't the opinion.`,
          `Purpose asks why the author wrote a passage or a paragraph. Main idea asks what it says. Function asks what one piece is doing for the whole: giving a reason, answering an objection, setting up a contrast, closing the matter. Organization asks how it's built, so watch the order, and who chose it. Courts often take issues in the order the lawyer raised them. That's the lawyer's order, and it tells you nothing about what the court thought mattered most.`,
          `Meaning in context is the one people miss, because they already know the word. The question isn't what the word means in a dictionary. It's what it means in that sentence. Standing can mean upright, or rank, or a person's right to bring a case at all, and only the sentence around it can tell you which. When a question asks what the court would agree with, stay inside what the court said. A court that calls evidence sufficient has said a jury could find something. It hasn't said the jury had to.`],
        terms: [[`Affirm`,`An appeals court's word for letting the judgment below stand.`],[`Meaning in context`,`What a word or phrase means in its own sentence and passage, which can differ from its usual meaning.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask for the main point, the primary purpose of the passage or a paragraph, what a phrase most nearly means as it's used, the function of a sentence, how the passage is organized, and what the author would most likely agree with.` },
      { h: `Writing it`,
        body: [`The writing part opens eight days before the test window, and you do it at home. You can do it after the test too, but nobody sees your score until it's done, so do it first. Fifty minutes. Fifteen are for reading and planning, and you can move on after five, which I wouldn't do. Thirty five are for writing. You get an issue people really do disagree about and three or four perspectives on it, a few sentences each. You take a position and answer some of the others.`,
          `Answering them is the job. Anybody can say what they think. The essay worth reading takes the strongest thing the other side said, says it fairly, and then says why it doesn't settle the question, or how much of it you'd keep. If your essay could have been written without reading the perspectives, you didn't do the task.`,
          `It isn't scored, and it still counts. Your score won't be released until an approved sample is on file, and the schools read it. No paper, no pencil. You type your notes into a box on the screen, with a camera on you and nothing on the table but your ID, tissues, foam earplugs and a clear bottle of water with the label peeled off.`,
          `One note on the video below. It was made before the test moved into test centers, so skip its tip about remote proctors. And LSAC sends schools every score you have on file, not just your highest one.`],
        terms: [[`Perspective`,`One of the short viewpoints that come with the writing prompt. You take your own position and engage some of them.`],[`Concession`,`Granting a point to another view before you answer it.`]],
        exam: `LSAC calls it LSAT Argumentative Writing. You can start it eight days before the test window opens, at home, on your own laptop or desktop with a webcam. Do it early. Processing can take a few weeks, and nobody sees your score until it's through.` }
    ],
    video: [`lmuxeOOMw-U`,`7 LSAT Test Day Mistakes That Destroy Scores (and How to Avoid Them)`,`LSAT Unplugged & Law School Admissions Podcast`,`8:13`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f9s1`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR03`, title:`Its share`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana set the kitchen timer to thirty five minutes and laid it face down on the desk. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Saturday you're going to hit one that won't let go of you,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Section two, probably. That's where the nerves wear off and the pride comes in. You'll read it three times. You'll be sure you're one more read from it."</span>`,
        `She wrote on a card in capitals and turned it around. EVERY QUESTION COUNTS THE SAME. A WRONG ANSWER COSTS NOTHING A BLANK WOULDN'T. THE ONE IN FRONT OF YOU IS WORTH WHAT THE EASY ONE THREE DOWN IS WORTH. SO.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Finish it,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Out loud."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"So I skip every hard one the second I see it, and I don't look at any of them until the very end."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Every one?"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Some hard ones are two reads from done, and you find that out by reading. Flag the one that's eating you. Not every one that looks at you funny."</span>`,
          why:`Complete the argument. Half right. Moving on is supported, but skipping every hard one on sight goes past the card. The premises are about what a question is worth. They say nothing about telling how hard it is at a glance.`},
        {t:`"So it gets its fair share of the clock and not a second more. Then I flag it and go get the easy one."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana turns the timer back over without starting it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Its share,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Say that to yourself in there. A question doesn't get more of your clock for being rude to you."</span>`,
          why:`Complete the argument. Every question is worth the same, and the minutes are shared among them. What those premises support is a rule about time: a hard question gets a fair share and no more, and you move on and come back. Nothing on the card says skip on sight, and nothing says hard ones don't count.`},
        {t:`"So I do the hard ones first, while I'm fresh, and I let the easy ones carry the end of every section."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Fresh for what?"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You'd spend your best minutes on your worst odds. Same points either way, remember. Go where the points are cheap."</span>`,
          why:`Complete the argument. Reversed. If every question pays the same, nothing favors spending your freshest time on the ones least likely to pay. The card points the other way.`},
        {t:`"So the hard ones are probably in the section that doesn't count, and I can let them all go without a fight."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You won't know which section doesn't count,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nobody does. Hard won't tell you. Treat every one of them like it's the one they're counting."</span>`,
          why:`Complete the argument. Unsupported, and wrong about the test. The card says nothing about which section is unscored, and you can't tell from how hard a section is.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s2`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC01`, title:`Nine in`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, June 2, at half past five, she wrote it at the kitchen table. She'd cleared the table to the wood. The laptop, her license, a water bottle with the label peeled off, and a dish towel taped over the window in the back door, because the rules said no glass. The camera made her show it the whole room, slowly, all the way around: the stove, the calendar, the basement door shut, and the hook by the back door where her mother's lanyard still hung, the hospital badge turned to the wall. Then fifteen minutes to read and plan, and thirty five to write, and for the first time since October no pencil in her hand.`,
        `At seven her father called. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"What'd they have you write about?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I can't tell you, Pa. I agreed not to. I clicked a box."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Good,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Don't."</span> He sounded pleased, a man who'd lived by a rule card since 2008. The next Wednesday he asked who was driving her Saturday, and she told him, and he asked her to say it again. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He drives careful. He always did."</span> And: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"What do you two talk about, all that way in a truck?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Nothing, Pa. Same as always."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Okay,"</span> he said, and she couldn't tell what kind of okay it was. Neither of them said a word about the board. It was her move, and had been since his queen went home, and he didn't ask for it.`,
        `Thursday, June 10, Ana opened her top drawer and took out the copy Dani had seen once, in October. Stapled, soft as a dish towel, pencil down every margin, a coffee ring on the back. She laid it open on the desk to the first page and then to the last.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Colorado Court of Appeals, opinion announced Thursday, June 17, 2010, page one">Defendant, Luis Ruiz, appeals the judgment of conviction entered on a jury verdict finding him guilty of second degree murder. He raises nine contentions. He argues that the trial court erred in instructing the jury, in declining to excuse a juror who may have fallen asleep during testimony, in admitting the victim's telephone statement to his sister as an excited utterance, and in five other rulings, and that the evidence was insufficient to identify him as the person who struck the victim. We address the first eight in the order he presents them. We affirm.</prop>`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="The same opinion, page eleven">IX. Remaining Contentions<br>Defendant's remaining contentions require little discussion. Taken in the light most favorable to the People, the evidence that defendant wore a red work jacket that night, that he had fought with the victim inside the bar less than an hour before, and that the victim's blood was on his cuff was sufficient for the jury to find that he was the man the witness saw strike the victim. The judgment is affirmed.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Last passage,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You've read yours. Read mine once like it's a stranger's. Seventeen years next Thursday."</span> She didn't look at it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Main point. One sentence."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The evidence was enough for a jury to find he was the man the witness saw strike the victim.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's page eleven,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"One issue out of nine. A main point has to cover the other ten pages too, and there's a lot of them."</span>`,
          why:`Main idea. Too narrow. That's the court's answer to the ninth contention only. The main point has to cover the whole opinion: all nine issues and the result.`},
        {t:`The trial court got a few of its rulings wrong, but none was bad enough to change the result.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Show me where they found an error,"</span> Ana says. Dani can't. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They didn't say harmless. They said no. There's a difference, and I've got ten pages of it."</span>`,
          why:`Main idea. Unsupported. The excerpts never say the trial court erred. They say the court rejects his contentions and affirms.`},
        {t:`The court turned down all nine of his contentions and let the conviction stand.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Affirmed,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nine in, nine out."</span> That's the point, she says, and it's the first word anybody reads on the thing and the only one anybody remembers. She takes her hand off the page.`,
          why:`Main idea. Page one sets out nine contentions and says the court affirms, and page eleven disposes of the last of them and affirms. The point that covers the whole opinion is that none of the nine succeeded and the conviction stands.`},
        {t:`The evidence proved he was the man who struck the victim outside the bar that night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana says proved back to her the way she says adjectives. Read the verb, she says. Sufficient for the jury to find. An appeals court doesn't decide who did it. It decides whether the jury was allowed to.`,
          why:`Main idea. Too strong. Proved means it happened. The court said something smaller: twelve people were allowed to look at that jacket, that fight and that blood and find it was him. Whether he actually struck anyone outside the bar is a question the opinion never answers, and it was never the court's to answer.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s3`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC09`, title:`In the order`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana turned through the pages in between one at a time, slowly, wetting her thumb on the corners like a bank teller. The jury instruction took two and a half pages. The juror took two, the call to Shannon took two more, and the five issues after that got shorter and shorter, down to a paragraph or so apiece. Then page eleven, one paragraph at the top and nothing under it but white paper.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"How's it built?"</span> she said.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It starts with his strongest argument and works its way down to the weakest, which gets the end.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Ana asks her where it says strongest. It doesn't say it anywhere. Ana says it looks that way because the long ones come first and the short ones come last, and all that tells you is the order somebody typed them in.`,
          why:`Organization. The passage says the court takes the issues in the order the defendant presents them. Nothing says that order runs from strongest to weakest, however the page counts look.`},
        {t:`It tells the story of the night first, then the trial, and after that it turns to his arguments.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana says there's no story anywhere on those two pages. It opens on what he argued and closes on what they decided, and the night itself never comes into it.`,
          why:`Organization. Not in the passage. The excerpts open with the appeal and the contentions, with no account of the night or the trial.`},
        {t:`It names nine, takes eight one at a time in the order he raised them, then ends the last in a paragraph.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana reads the line out loud, in the order he presents them, and taps it with the pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He didn't present anything,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I did. That's my order, all eleven pages of it, and they walked it exactly the way I laid it out in the fall of 2009."</span>`,
          why:`Organization. Page one names nine contentions and says the court will take the first eight in the order presented. Page eleven handles what remains in one paragraph and affirms. That's the structure.`},
        {t:`It argues every issue from both sides, his and the State's, and then rules on all nine at the end.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They don't argue the State's side,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They decide. And they don't save it all for the end. Each one gets its answer where it sits."</span>`,
          why:`Organization. Unsupported. The excerpts show the court stating his arguments and ruling on them. They don't show it arguing both sides or holding every ruling until the end.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s4`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC07`, title:`Remaining`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana laid the eraser end of her pencil on the desk just under page eleven, below the two words at the top of it, careful not to touch the paper. Remaining contentions.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Meaning in context,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not what it means. What it means there."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The arguments he was still making on appeal, after he'd let go of some of the others.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Still making,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's what remaining means at plenty of tables. Not this one. Nobody let go of anything. Read page one again. Nine in."</span>`,
          why:`Meaning in context. Remaining can mean still being pressed, but nothing in the passage says he dropped an argument. Page one lists nine, and remaining refers to the ones the court hadn't reached yet.`},
        {t:`His strongest arguments, the ones a court saves for the end because they matter most.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana laughs, one short sound with nothing funny in it, and says that if it had meant that, they'd have given it more than a sentence.`,
          why:`Meaning in context. Unsupported. Nothing in the passage ties remaining to importance, and one sentence of discussion points the other way.`},
        {t:`The questions still open after the trial, the ones the jury never got the chance to decide.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana says the jury decided all of it, which is what a verdict is for. This is about what was still left of his appeal, on this page, in 2010.`,
          why:`Meaning in context. In this opinion the phrase is about what he argued to the Court of Appeals, the list on page one. Questions left over from the trial aren't what it means.`},
        {t:`Whatever he'd argued that the court hadn't already taken up in the first ten pages.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Left over,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Like the coffee in the pot at the end of the day."</span> Then she says, to the page and not to Dani, that it's a polite word, and courts keep a lot of those around.`,
          why:`Meaning in context. Page one says the court will take up the first eight contentions, and page eleven begins with the remaining ones. Set there, remaining means the ones left over after the first eight.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s5`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC08`, title:`One sentence`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Before she left that night Ana gave her two things. The copy, still open to page eleven, which as far as Dani knew had never once left the drawer. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Bring it back when you're done with it."</span> And a stapled report, eight pages. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Practice set for tomorrow night. Three more questions on the opinion, I wrote them down for you. Then this. Then bed."</span> She'd sent the report to a pathologist in Denver the week before. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Before she calls me and tells me what she thinks it says, I want you to have read it yourself."</span>`,
        `Friday, June 11, she had the opinion on the kitchen table with the lamp pulled close. Ana's card was clipped to the front, three questions in capitals. WHAT'S THE ONE SENTENCE DOING? WHY WRITE THE LAST PARAGRAPH AT ALL? WHAT WOULD THEY AGREE WITH?`,
        `Beside the sentence, in pencil gone gray, somebody had written a small 1 and put a circle around it. She knew whose pencil. She didn't know what year.`,
        `The first question, then.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It gives the court's only reason for rejecting his claim that the evidence didn't identify him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`The only reason, and it's three facts in one sentence with and between them. She reads it once more with a pencil she doesn't need. Jacket, fight, blood. The court never says how many men wore that jacket. The sentence works without anybody saying it, and that's the trouble with the sentence.`,
          why:`Function of a detail. The sentence takes the identification challenge, the last of the nine, and answers it: the jacket, the fight and the blood were enough for a jury. It's the court's reason for rejecting that claim.`},
        {t:`It lists three pieces of the evidence against him that the jury heard at the trial.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It does list them. But a list isn't a job. The sentence is there to answer his ninth contention, and the list is how it answers.`,
          why:`Function of a detail. It does name evidence from the trial, but describing that evidence isn't its purpose. The sentence uses it to reject a specific argument.`},
        {t:`It admits that the identification was the weakest part of the case, and then it decides it anyway.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She looks for the admission and there isn't one. The court doesn't call anything weak. It calls the evidence sufficient and goes on to the last line.`,
          why:`Function of a detail. Unsupported. Nothing in the sentence concedes any weakness.`},
        {t:`It sets out the rule the court used to decide all nine of his contentions together at once.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`All nine? The first eight got ten pages and answers of their own. This sentence is about who struck the victim and nothing else.`,
          why:`Function of a detail. The sentence addresses only the identification issue. Page one says the first eight are taken up one at a time.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s6`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC02`, title:`At all`, q:`What's the primary purpose of page eleven?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`WHY WRITE THE LAST PARAGRAPH AT ALL? Ana had put a pencil line under AT ALL.`,
        `It was a fair question. The court had spent ten pages on eight arguments. It could have stopped at the bottom of page ten, and it wrote one more paragraph instead.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`To show other courts that the identification issue was the most serious one in the whole appeal.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The most serious one would have gotten more than a sentence. Nothing in the paragraph ranks anything.`,
          why:`Primary purpose. Unsupported, and the reverse of what the passage suggests. One sentence after ten pages on other issues doesn't signal importance.`},
        {t:`To explain why the trial evidence was enough for a jury to find he was the man in the red jacket.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`That's what the middle sentence does. The paragraph does more. It closes the whole appeal, and the identification answer is only the last thing in it.`,
          why:`Primary purpose. Too narrow. That's the purpose of one sentence in it. The paragraph as a whole disposes of the remaining contentions and ends the case.`},
        {t:`To answer whatever was left of the appeal, so the ruling covered every argument before it said affirmed.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`That's the job a last paragraph does in an opinion like this. You can't affirm with an argument still sitting there unanswered. So it got answered, in a sentence, because a sentence was all it took.`,
          why:`Primary purpose. The paragraph disposes of the one contention the opinion hadn't yet reached, identification, and then states the result. It's there to finish the appeal, so the affirmance covers everything he raised.`},
        {t:`To scold his lawyer, gently, for burying a court under nine arguments when one would have done.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Nine issues tells a judge you don't believe any of them. Ana had said that in March, to her whiteboard. The court never said it. Ana did.`,
          why:`Primary purpose. Not in the passage. The court says nothing about how many issues were raised or how they were argued.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s7`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC04`, title:`Sufficient`, q:`Which statement is the court committed to by what it wrote on page eleven?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`WHAT WOULD THEY AGREE WITH? The last question on the card. She held page eleven under the lamp and read the sentence slowly, the way she'd read her own testimony on the bus in October.`,
        ((s.flags.overclaimed||G.overclaimed)
          ?`Sufficient. In March she'd written proves in a draft, and Ana had circled it twice. Three judges in 2010 had been more careful with their verb than she'd been with hers.`
          :`Sufficient. Of all the words they could have used, they'd used that one.`)],
      opts:[
        {t:`The jacket was the most important piece of evidence the jury had for finding that he was the man.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`First in the list isn't first in importance. They gave all three facts the same and.`,
          why:`Would agree. The court lists the jacket among three facts, joined the same way, and never ranks them. Most important goes past the sentence.`},
        {t:`A jury may rely on several facts taken together to find that the defendant struck the victim.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Three things joined by and, and a jury allowed to add them up. That much they'd sign. How many men the first of the three fit, the sentence never says, and it reads just fine without saying it.`,
          why:`Would agree. The court held that the jacket, the fight and the blood together were sufficient for the jury to find identity. It's committed to the idea that a jury may combine several facts to reach that finding.`},
        {t:`No one else who was at the bar that night had on a red work jacket like the one he wore.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`They never said it, and the sentence doesn't need it. With the fight and the blood next to it, the jacket never has to be the only one. That's the part that bothers her.`,
          why:`Would agree. Not in the passage. The opinion never addresses whether anyone else wore the jacket.`},
        {t:`The witness had a clear enough look at the man's face to be certain that it was him at the curb.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The page says the man the witness saw. It doesn't say what she saw of him, and Dani knew from February exactly how little that was.`,
          why:`Would agree. Not in the passage. The opinion says the witness saw a man strike the victim, and nothing about seeing his face.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s8`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR04`, title:`One more`, q:`Her plan in the log depends on which assumption?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Then the report. She'd had every page of the case in her hands by now except this one. There was a copy in Phil's second banker's box too, and she'd lifted it out and set it aside twice, the way you step around a soft place in a floor.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Report of autopsy, Travis Hensley, October 28, 2007, from the findings and the opinion">Laceration, 2.5 cm, above the left eyebrow. Superficial. Does not reach the bone. Bruising of the left brow and upper eyelid.<br>Depressed fracture of the back of the skull, with bleeding over and into the brain.<br>Fingernail scrapings and clippings collected from both hands, released to Det. G. Vigil.<br>Opinion. Cause of death: blunt force injury of the head. The fatal injury is the depressed fracture at the back of the skull. The laceration above the left eyebrow is a separate, superficial injury and is non-contributory. Manner of death: homicide.</prop>`,
        `She read it the way Ana said to, like a passage. Main point, the fracture killed him. Detail, two and a half centimeters. Meaning in context, non-contributory: it was there, and it had nothing to do with how it ended. She wrote the answers in the margin in pencil and they were right, and she sat there with them anyway.`,
        `Thirty one years old. Six foot two, page one said. A sheet metal worker, a Rockies fan having a bad night. She'd known his name since she was fifteen and had never once read where he was hurt.${(s.flags.wroteshannon||G.wroteshannon)?` She'd written his sister two pages in May without reading this.`:``}`,
        `At a quarter to eleven she had LawHub open on the laptop, and the full practice test she'd been saving, and the log on her phone. This week she'd missed more than any week since March. She typed it into the log. Worst week since March. Not ready. One more full test tonight and I'll know where I stand in the morning.`,
        `She read it back like it was somebody else's.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That this week's misses came from not knowing the material, and not from a week of bad sleep.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Maybe. It isn't the hole, though. Even if every miss was hers fair and square, the plan still needs tonight's test to say something about tomorrow, and it's eleven o'clock. She does one section instead of four and goes to bed at half past twelve.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. It bears on whether she's ready, but the conclusion is about what tonight's test would tell her. The plan needs tonight to predict tomorrow, whatever caused the misses.`},
        {t:`That a full test she starts at eleven tonight would tell her how she'll do at eight tomorrow.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Deny it and the plan falls over. If a test at eleven can't tell her about eight, it isn't worth the sleep. She shuts the laptop. She sets two alarms and puts the phone across the room, and lies there until one, which beats four.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. The plan says one more test tonight will tell her where she stands tomorrow. That depends on tonight's tired score predicting tomorrow's rested one. Negate it, and the reason for the test is gone.`},
        {t:`Nothing she can find. She starts the full test at eleven, and when it's done she starts another.`, s:0, flag:`crammed`, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The first one ends at a quarter to two. She does worse than her log says she should, which tells her she needs the second one. It ends after four, with the sky going gray over the roofs across the street. She sleeps two hours on top of the covers.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Not an answer, and the night before is a bad time to skip the question. The plan assumes a test at eleven predicts a test at eight, which is the one thing it can't show.`},
        {t:`That she'd have missed fewer this week if Ana had handed her more practice tests to work on.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana's got nothing to do with it. Blaming the supply doesn't touch the plan. She does one section to make a point to herself and goes to bed after midnight and can't remember the point.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Irrelevant. Who handed out the practice tests has nothing to do with whether tonight's test would predict tomorrow.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR18`, title:`Four seconds`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[((s.flags.crammed||G.crammed)
          ?`Saturday, June 12. Two hours of sleep, maybe, and her eyes felt sanded. She was in the shower when the phone buzzed on the sink.`
          :`Saturday, June 12. She'd slept some, and she was up before the alarm. She was making coffee she didn't need when the phone buzzed on the counter.`),
        `<prop type="text" from="Prometric" time="6:02 AM">Reminder: your appointment is Saturday, June 12, at 8:00 AM, Greenwood Village. Please arrive 30 minutes early with a valid government issued photo ID.</prop>`,
        `At 6:25 the truck was at the curb, twenty minutes early. He'd washed it. She could see where the water had dried in streaks on the hood. Two coffees in the cup holders, one with her name on the lid in Bianca's writing, a burrito in foil on the dash, and under the burrito a folded sheet with a purple horse on it holding a pencil. GOOD LUK TIA.`,
        `He said morning. She said morning. Then 270, and 25 south with the sun already up and low over the plains on their left, the radio low on a station that was mostly ads, and her brother not saying one thing, his hands at ten and two.${(s.flags.pushedteo||G.pushedteo)?` Since the party he'd had a carefulness around her, like a man carrying something full to the brim. It was there now.`:``}`,
        `Five weeks he hadn't answered the text about Pa. Then yeah. in four seconds, and now this. She looked at the lid with her name on it and tried to get both facts to sit in the same truck.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Work had him on doubles at the bus garage for five weeks, and this week it finally let up on him.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Doubles would explain the quiet. Nothing about doubles puts you at the curb twenty minutes early with two coffees.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Doubles letting up this week can't explain a yes in four seconds back in May, and doubles never stopped anybody sending one word. It leaves the quick yes as strange as it was.`},
        {t:`Pa's the thing he can't talk about. A ride he can say yes to, and drive without saying a word.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`So she doesn't bring up Pa. She doesn't bring up anything. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"You washed the truck."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"It was dirty."</span> Past the Broadway exit he reaches over and turns the radio down one more notch.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. The two facts are a long silence after a text about their father and a fast yes to a ride. If what he can't do is talk about Luis, and the ride asks nothing of him but driving, both facts fit at once.`},
        {t:`He feels bad about the party and the pawn text, and this is how he's trying to make it up to her.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`Maybe. But sorry explains the yes and not the five weeks. She'd have heard sorry by now, some kind of it. He isn't a man who says it. He's a man who washes the truck.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Half of it. Guilt could explain the quick yes, but it leaves the five weeks of silence just as strange as before.`},
        {t:`Bianca made him say yes and wash the truck, the way she makes him go to Lupe's school things.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`It was his thumb on the phone at 9:41, not Bianca's, and four seconds is faster than anybody gets talked into anything. She lets it go and drinks the coffee with her name on the lid.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Even if Bianca pushed him, that wouldn't account for an answer in four seconds, or for the five weeks of nothing before it, so neither fact gets explained.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR06`, title:`Ten minutes`, q:`Her reasoning at the cooler is most vulnerable to which criticism?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The test center was in an office building in the Tech Center, beige carpet and a water cooler and a row of chairs, and she was the third one there. She left the prep book on the truck seat. Inside it would only have gone in a locker. The envelope was in it, marking her page. She knew it was. She thought about taking it out, and didn't.`,
        `A man behind a counter looked at her license and then at her, a second each. Phone off, into a gray locker with Lupe's horse, the burrito and her water. Her username and password she said to herself once more, as she had on the bus all week. They gave her a station by the wall with a computer on it, gray earmuffs the size of a pilot's, two pencils and a short stack of scratch paper, and there was a camera up in the corner of the ceiling. She read the statement on the screen and agreed to it. Then section one came up, with its clock running in the corner.`,
        `Section two had a question in it about a ferry schedule that she read three times and couldn't make give. She flagged it and went on. With four minutes left she came back, read it once more, saw it, and changed her answer. Eraser marks, she thought, and moved on.`,
        `Then the break. Ten minutes on a timer. She told the proctor, waited for the nod, and walked out to the lockers for her water, past the phone she couldn't touch. A young guy at the next locker was shaking his hands out. <span class="said" data-who="man">"That last section,"</span> he said to her. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Brutal. That's got to be the experimental, right?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I can't talk about it,"</span> she said. He looked at her like she'd said it in Latin, then like he remembered, and went off toward the bathroom.`,
        ((s.flags.crammed||G.crammed)
          ?`Her head was full of cotton. Section two had been the worst forty minutes she'd had since October, and standing at the cooler she thought the thing she'd told Ana she wouldn't think.`
          :`Section two had been the worst of it, by a lot. Standing at the cooler, she caught herself thinking the thing she'd told Ana she wouldn't think.`),
        `That one was the hardest. So that's the one that doesn't count. So I'm fine.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It assumes section two went badly, when she flagged only one question and got back to it in time.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Maybe it went fine. That's a different argument, and she can't check it from here. The broken step is the one from hard to doesn't count.`,
          why:`Flaw. It questions the premise, that section two went badly. The reasoning has a worse problem even if the premise is true: hard doesn't mean unscored.`},
        {t:`It forgets a wrong answer costs nothing, so a hard section can't hurt her score at all.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`A wrong answer costs nothing next to a blank. It still isn't a right one. A hard section that counts can cost her plenty.`,
          why:`Flaw. No penalty for wrong answers means you should guess. It doesn't mean a hard section can't cost points. Misses on a scored section still lower the score.`},
        {t:`It reads which section doesn't count off how hard one felt, and the test never tells anybody that.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Hard is a feeling she had at a water cooler. Which section doesn't count is a fact nobody in that building knows. So section two counted, as far as she's concerned, and three would, and four. She finishes the water, sits back down with two minutes to spare, and waits with her hands in her lap for the timer to run out.`,
          why:`Flaw. Her reasoning uses difficulty as evidence that section two was the unscored one. The extra section could be the easiest of the morning or the worst, and nothing on the screen marks it, so how it felt tells her nothing about whether it counts.`},
        {t:`Nothing's wrong with it. If everybody in the room found section two hard, they're all in one boat.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`That answers a different worry. Whether everybody found it hard says nothing about whether it counts.`,
          why:`Flaw. It doesn't find the error. How other people did has nothing to do with whether this section is scored.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR10`, title:`One minute`, q:`What do those facts most strongly support about the three she hasn't opened?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Section three was Reading Comprehension, and it went like the last eight months said it would. Section four didn't. Twenty three questions in, the clock in the corner said 1:00, and there were three she hadn't opened.`,
        `Every question worth the same. Nothing off for a wrong one. One minute.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`One of the three is probably easy enough that she could still work it out inside a minute.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Probably isn't a minute's worth of information. She opens the first one and the clock eats twenty seconds while she reads. She fills all three at 0:09, which is cutting it close.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. It might be true, but nothing given says how hard the three are. The facts support filling them in. They don't support a guess about which one she could solve.`},
        {t:`Guessing on all three will raise her score on this section by at least one question.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She wants it to. A guess might be right, and at one chance in five it probably isn't. She fills them anyway, for the reason that's actually true.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Too strong. A guess can't hurt her, but with five choices each, three guesses can easily all miss.`},
        {t:`Filling in all three can only tie a blank or beat it, never do worse.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She clicks the same letter on all three. Then she opens the last one and reads the stem, because there are forty seconds, and forty seconds is a stem. She changes nothing. The section ends before she's sure it has.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. With no deduction for wrong answers and every question worth the same, a guess can only tie a blank or beat it. That's all the facts support. They don't promise a gain.`},
        {t:`She should pick whichever letter she's used the least this section, since it's due.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Letters aren't due. Each answer was set by whoever wrote that question, not by the ten before it. She fills them in, though, and that part's right.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Unsupported. Nothing about earlier answers tells you which letter comes next.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f9s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR09`, title:`By myself`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`It was twenty past twelve and ninety degrees on the asphalt when she came out, and she had no idea how she'd done. She knew she couldn't say a word about it to anybody. The phone came back to life in her hand with one text, from that morning.`,
        `<prop type="text" from="Ana" time="7:58 AM">Pencil down when they tell you. Then go eat.</prop>`,
        `The truck was in the far corner of the lot under the one tree, with the engine running for the air. Teo had the prep book on the dash. The envelope wasn't in it. It was in his hand, back side up. WHO WAS THE KID? in her capitals, and under it his name.`,
        `She'd left it there. She'd known it was in the book when she shut the truck door on it, and she'd let it ride on the seat beside him all morning. He'd found it early, she could tell. He'd sat out here under the tree with it the whole time, so it wouldn't be in that room with her.`,
        ((s.flags.pushedteo||G.pushedteo)
          ?`He didn't ask how it went. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You put my name on it,"</span> he said, quiet and flat, the voice from the carport in March. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"At the party you wanted to know what I wasn't telling you. Okay."</span> He slapped the envelope flat against the wheel. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I was there, Dani."</span>`
          :`He didn't ask how it went. He held the envelope out so she'd see he'd seen it, and then didn't let go of it. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You put my name on it,"</span> he said. Not angry. Tired, like a man who'd sat four hours with it, which he had. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I was there, Dani."</span>`),
        `She got in and shut the door, and the cold air hit her. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"In Pa's truck,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"When he drove out of that lot, I was in it."</span>`,
        `Pa had said it to Gene in 2007, and to her on a hundred Wednesdays, and across a cardboard board in April. Went home. By myself.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Then Pa's lied to me about that whole night, Teo. Every word he ever said."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Not every word,"</span> Teo says, low. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Don't do that. Don't do the whole thing at once."</span> He sounds like their mother saying it, and he hears it, and stops.`,
          why:`Must be true. Too strong. One false claim doesn't make every other claim false. It's the step she nearly took at Sterling, from one lie to all of them.`},
        {t:`"Then you know where Pa went after he left. You were right there with him."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`He says he was ten. He says he knew where they went. He doesn't say where. It's probably true. It isn't the thing that has to be true, and it isn't what he's been sitting out here four hours to say.`,
          why:`Must be true. Likely, but not required. A boy of ten riding in a truck at night might sleep the whole way, or not know the streets, so knowing where his father went doesn't follow. What has to follow is narrower.`},
        {t:`"Then Pa didn't drive home by himself. That part isn't true."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Teo says that part, yes. He says it to the envelope. Two rows over a woman in hospital scrubs gets out of an old Corolla, and a girl comes out of the building with her license still in her hand, and the woman hugs her right there between the cars, and Dani watches them instead of her brother.`,
          why:`Must be true. Since 2007 Luis has said he drove home by himself, and now Teo puts himself in that truck when it pulled out. If Teo's right about that, then by himself can't be. Nothing else has to follow from what he said.`},
        {t:`"So you were there when that man got hit at the curb. You saw Pa."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`Teo tells her no, too fast and too loud for the cab of a truck, and then he tells her again with her name on the end of it. He puts both hands on the wheel. They're parked. Their father used to sit like that in the driveway after a double, engine off, before he came in to eat.`,
          why:`Must be true. Unsupported. Being in the truck when it left says nothing about who was at the curb at a quarter past eleven.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f9s1`, title:`Which one answers somebody`, intro:`Ana's practice prompt, one typed page, the issue at the top and four short perspectives under it. Should the city put cameras at every bus stop? One: cameras stop crimes and solve them. Two: people waiting for a bus shouldn't be filmed going about their lives. Three: a camera is a witness that doesn't guess, and footage clears people as often as it convicts them. Four: the money would do more as streetlights and a person in a booth. Before Wednesday, Ana wanted the pieces of an essay picked out one card at a time.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. Which opening takes a position?`, choices:[`Cameras at bus stops raise hard questions about safety and privacy.`,`The city should put cameras at its busiest stops first and post the rules for the footage at every one.`,`Some people say cameras help and others say they hurt, and both sides have a point.`], answer:1, why:`The first describes the debate, and the third stays on the fence. The middle one says what the city should do, and how far. A position can be partial. It still has to be a position.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. Which sentence engages perspective two instead of brushing past it?`, choices:[`Privacy matters, but safety matters more.`,`Nobody has a right to privacy at a public bus stop.`,`The privacy worry is strongest where footage is kept for months, so the city should erase it after thirty days unless a crime is reported.`], answer:2, why:`The first waves at privacy and moves on. The second dismisses it with a claim nobody can check. The third takes the worry seriously and changes the plan because of it. That's engaging.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. Which one uses perspective three as evidence instead of decoration?`, choices:[`As one perspective says, a camera is a witness.`,`Footage of who stood at a stop at eleven at night can clear the person a tired witness picked out, which is a reason to want cameras that has nothing to do with catching anyone.`,`Cameras are a good idea for a lot of reasons.`], answer:1, why:`The first quotes it and stops. The third says nothing at all. The middle one explains what the point shows and why it matters to the position.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. Which concession is fair to perspective four?`, choices:[`Streetlights and a person in a booth cost less, and on some blocks they'd do more good than any camera.`,`Some people think lights are better, but they're wrong.`,`Lights cost money too, so that point fails.`], answer:0, why:`A fair concession grants what's true in the other view before answering it. The second is a dismissal. The third is a dodge, since nobody said lights were free.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. Ana's prewriting question: what's the strongest thing anyone said against cameras?`, choices:[`That cameras cost money.`,`That people filmed every day at a bus stop change how they act there, even when nothing ever happens.`,`That some people just don't like cameras.`], answer:1, why:`Strongest means the one that would still bother you after you'd answered it. Cost has an answer. A dislike is a feeling. A city that changes how people act at its own bus stops has paid a real price, and the essay has to face it.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. Which last sentence belongs at the end of the essay?`, choices:[`In conclusion, cameras are a complicated issue with many sides.`,`The city should start with the ten stops with the most reported crimes, erase the footage nothing happened on, and look again in a year.`,`Only time will tell whether cameras are the answer.`], answer:1, why:`The first and the last could end any essay ever written. The middle one could only end this one. Say what you'd do, and stop there.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Report of autopsy, Travis Hensley, October 28, 2007, excerpt`,
      text:[`The body is that of an adult male, 31 years of age, 74 inches in length and 210 pounds, received from the hospital, where death was pronounced at 1:40 AM on October 28, 2007, after treatment for a head injury.`,
        `External examination. There is a laceration, 2.5 cm in length, above the left eyebrow. It is superficial and does not reach the bone. There is bruising of the left brow and upper eyelid. Fingernail scrapings and clippings are collected from both hands and released to Detective G. Vigil, Commerce City Police Department.`,
        `Internal examination of the head. There is a depressed fracture of the back of the skull, with bleeding over and into the brain.`,
        `Opinion. The cause of death is blunt force injury of the head. The fatal injury is the depressed fracture at the back of the skull. The laceration above the left eyebrow is a separate, superficial injury and is non-contributory. The manner of death is homicide.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: eraser marks`,
      text:[`Ask a room of students whether to change an answer on a multiple choice test, and most will tell you not to. Surveys of college students going back to 1929 found that about three in four believed changing answers tended to lower scores, and test preparation guides have long repeated the advice to trust your first instinct. The research has mostly said the opposite. Since the 1920s, study after study has found that when people change an answer, they more often move from a wrong answer to a right one than the reverse.`,
        `In 2005 the psychologists Justin Kruger, Derrick Wirtz and Dale Miller asked why the belief survives the evidence, and they began by counting. They gathered the midterm exams of 1,561 students in an introductory psychology course at the University of Illinois, taken in the fall of 2000, and had trained coders look for eraser marks. Of the 3,291 answers that had been changed, 51 percent went from wrong to right, 25 percent from right to wrong, and 23 percent from one wrong answer to another. Of the students who changed at least one answer, more than half came out ahead and fewer than one in five came out behind. Yet a randomly chosen group of those same students, asked to guess the results, expected the changes from right to wrong to outnumber the ones that helped.`,
        `The explanation the researchers proposed turned on memory. Changing a right answer to a wrong one stings in a particular way: you had it, you gave it away, and it is easy to picture having done otherwise. Keeping a wrong answer you might have fixed stings less, because you did nothing, and the mistake feels like the question's fault. In further studies, students said they would regret the first kind of error more, and weeks after a practice test they misremembered how often each had happened, recalling the costly switches and losing the costly stays. The painful cases come to mind first, so they seem to be the common ones.`,
        `The authors compared it to changing lines at a grocery store. Most people can recall switching lines and then watching the old one speed up. Fewer recall staying put and watching the other line move, though that happens too. The memory isn't false. It is a sample, and it was chosen by how bad each moment felt.`,
        `None of this means a test taker should change answers freely. The studies counted changes that people chose to make, and a change made on a whim, for no reason at all, may do no better than chance. What the work shows is narrower and more useful. A rule built from the cases a person remembers best can be wrong even when every one of those memories is accurate, and the cure is the dull one the researchers used: count the cases you'd rather forget along with the ones you can't.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`City engineer: Alder Park has flooded every spring for a decade because the storm drain under it was too small to carry the snowmelt that runs down from the foothills. Last fall the city replaced that drain with one twice its size. So the snowmelt that has flooded Alder Park every spring will not flood it this spring.<br><br>The engineer's argument depends on the assumption that`,
      opts:[`the crew that installed the new drain had built drains of that size before`,`this spring's snowmelt will be lighter than the snowmelt in most recent springs`,`a drain twice the size of the old one can carry the snowmelt that will reach the park`,`no other park in the city has flooded from snowmelt during the past decade`,`the city won't need to replace the new drain again within the next ten years`],
      a:2, why:`Necessary assumption. The old drain was too small, and the argument jumps from twice the size to big enough. Deny that a drain twice the size can carry this spring's snowmelt, and the park floods. A lighter spring would help, but the argument doesn't need one. The crew, other parks and a future replacement don't bear on the conclusion.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Critic: Wren Hollister's novels are the finest written by anyone in this state over the past decade. I have read every novel published here in that time, many of them twice, so I say this with some confidence. The reason is simple. Over those ten years, no novelist working in the state has written with greater skill than she has.<br><br>The reasoning in the critic's argument is flawed because the argument`,
      opts:[`offers as support a claim that amounts to a restatement of its conclusion`,`takes for granted that a critic who reads very widely cannot be mistaken about quality`,`treats the number of novels a critic has read as proof of their quality`,`assumes that the best novels of a decade are always the most popular`,`fails to consider that some of those novels were read only once`],
      a:0, why:`Flaw. Finest written and written with greater skill than anyone else are the same claim in different words, so the reason adds nothing to the conclusion. The critic's reading is offered for confidence, not as proof the critic can't be wrong or that quantity means quality. Popularity and rereading don't come into it.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Restaurant owner: My nephew wants to cook for a living, and I've told him the path is clear. Nearly every professional chef working in this city went to culinary school. So if he finishes culinary school next spring, he will almost certainly end up working here as a professional chef.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately describes a flaw in the owner's reasoning?`,
      opts:[`It relies on the owner's knowledge of the chefs in only one city.`,`It treats the nephew's wish to cook as if it guaranteed his talent.`,`It overlooks the possibility that some chefs in the city never went to culinary school.`,`It moves from most chefs being graduates to most graduates becoming chefs.`,`It assumes that the school the nephew attends is a good culinary school.`],
      a:3, why:`Flaw. Nearly all chefs went to culinary school, but that says nothing about what share of graduates become chefs. Thousands could graduate for a handful of jobs. The owner runs the relationship backward. The premise already allows for a few chefs without school, and the nephew's talent, the school's quality and the one city aren't what the conclusion rests on.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`At the Copper Run hatchery, trout raised in tanks fed by spring water grew faster over the summer than trout raised in tanks fed by river water. The two sets of tanks were the same size, held the same number of fish, and followed the same feeding schedule. The spring water stays near 12 degrees all year, while the river water ranged from 6 to 19 degrees over the summer.<br><br>Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,
      opts:[`Trout grow fastest in water that stays at exactly 12 degrees.`,`Some difference between the two water supplies affected the trout's growth.`,`River water carries diseases that slow the growth of young trout.`,`The hatchery should switch every one of its tanks to spring water.`,`Trout raised in river water are less healthy than trout raised in spring water.`],
      a:1, why:`Most strongly supported. The tanks matched in size, crowding and feeding, so the faster growth most likely came from the water, though the passage doesn't say which feature of it. Exactly 12 degrees and disease are causes the passage doesn't establish, should is a recommendation, and growth isn't health.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`After the Halden transit district added three buses to its busiest route, the average wait at each stop on that route fell from fourteen minutes to nine. Yet over the same months, the number of riders complaining that the route's buses were too crowded rose sharply.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?`,
      opts:[`The district's other routes saw no change in their average waiting times.`,`The new buses were the same size as the buses already running on the route.`,`Riders on the busiest route had complained about long waits for many years.`,`The district raised fares slightly on all of its routes that same spring.`,`The shorter waits drew many people who had been driving to ride the route.`],
      a:4, why:`Resolve the paradox. If shorter waits pulled in a lot of new riders, more buses and more crowding fit together. Other routes, the size of the new buses and old complaints about waiting don't explain the crowding. A fare increase would, if anything, cut ridership.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`At the Morrow Street clinic, patients who left with printed instructions from their doctor remembered more of those instructions a week later than patients who got the same instructions only out loud. But the patients with printed instructions were also more likely to phone the clinic with questions during that week.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the findings described above?`,
      opts:[`The clinic's printed instructions were written at a level most adults read easily.`,`Patients who got their instructions only out loud saw doctors with more years of practice.`,`Reading the instructions at home led patients to notice points they hadn't understood.`,`Most of the clinic's patients have a telephone at home that they can use to call.`,`Some patients who were given printed instructions lost them before the week ended.`],
      a:2, why:`Resolve the paradox. If patients reread the printed instructions and found parts they didn't understand, they'd remember more and call more. Easy reading explains the memory but not the calls. Experienced doctors for the other group would cut against the memory finding. Phones and lost papers don't explain both.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Some residents want the town to pave the gravel trail along Ketchum Creek so that people who use wheelchairs can reach the overlook. Others object that pavement would harm the creek, since rain running off a paved surface carries oil and silt into the water. But a trail of crushed stone, packed firm, can be crossed easily in a wheelchair, and rain soaks into it instead of running off. So the town ______.<br><br>Which one of the following most logically completes the argument?`,
      opts:[`should pave the trail, because wheelchair access matters more than the health of the creek`,`can open the trail to wheelchairs without causing the runoff the objectors fear`,`has no reason to worry about runoff from any kind of trail surface at all`,`will satisfy every resident who has an opinion about the Ketchum Creek trail`,`should leave the gravel trail as it is until both groups of residents agree`],
      a:1, why:`Complete the argument. Packed crushed stone gives the first group the access they want and avoids the runoff the second group fears, so the town can meet both concerns. Paving ignores the argument's own solution, any surface and every resident go too far, and waiting for agreement doesn't follow from anything given.`, task:`LR03`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The Cass County water board sets the rates the county's water utility charges, and it was created to oversee the utility from outside. Every member of the water board lives in the county. Some members of the water board are engineers. No engineer who lives in Cass County is employed by the county's water utility.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,
      opts:[`No member of the water board is employed by the county's water utility.`,`Most engineers who live in Cass County serve on the county's water board.`,`Some engineers employed by the water utility live outside of Cass County.`,`Some members of the water board aren't employed by the county's water utility.`,`Every engineer on the water board was appointed because of that training.`],
      a:3, why:`Must be true. Some board members are engineers, every board member lives in the county, and no engineer living in the county works for the utility. So those engineer members don't work for the utility. The other members might, so "no member" goes too far. The utility might employ no engineers at all, and nothing touches most engineers or why anyone was appointed.`, task:`LR09`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Many people assume that a bridge is safest when it is new. Yet the first years of a bridge's life are when hidden flaws in its steel or concrete are most likely to show themselves, which is why inspectors visit new bridges more often than old ones. A bridge that has stood twenty years without trouble has already been tested by twenty winters. The common assumption, then, has things largely backward.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?`,
      opts:[`The belief that bridges are safest when new largely has things backward.`,`Inspectors visit new bridges more often than they visit old ones.`,`A twenty year old bridge is safer than any newly built bridge.`,`Hidden flaws in a bridge most often show up in its first years.`,`Winter weather is the greatest danger a bridge faces in its life.`],
      a:0, why:`Main conclusion. The last sentence says the common assumption has things backward, and everything before it supports that. The early flaws and the inspection schedule are premises. Safer than any new bridge goes past largely, and nothing ranks winter as the greatest danger.`, task:`LR01`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Bakery owner: In January we began baking all of our bread with flour from a small mill thirty miles away, and since then our bread sales have risen by 30 percent. Our customers clearly prefer bread made from locally milled flour.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the owner's argument?`,
      opts:[`The mill's flour costs the bakery somewhat more than the flour it used before.`,`The bakery's menu board tells customers that its flour is milled locally.`,`Bread sales at the bakery had held steady through each of the previous five years.`,`Several regular customers have told the owner the new bread tastes better.`,`The only other bakery in the neighborhood closed its doors at the end of December.`],
      a:4, why:`Weaken. If the neighborhood's other bakery closed just before the switch, its customers had to buy bread somewhere, and that alone could explain the rise. Cost doesn't bear on preference, the menu board and the praise point the other way, and steady earlier sales make the January jump look more like the flour's doing.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Ines asked her neighbor's teenage son to shovel her front walk on Saturday and agreed to pay him twenty dollars. He shoveled it early that morning. By noon the sun had melted the snow on every walk on the street, shoveled or not. Ines says she shouldn't have to pay, since the walk would have been clear by noon anyway. Her neighbor disagrees.<br><br>Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the neighbor's position?`,
      opts:[`A person should pay for work only if it produced some result that wouldn't have come about otherwise.`,`Neighbors should help one another with chores around the house without expecting to be paid.`,`A person who agrees to pay for work should pay once it's done, even if it turns out to be unneeded.`,`A young worker should be paid more than an adult for the same job, as a form of encouragement.`,`What a person pays for outdoor work should depend on how hard the weather made the job that day.`],
      a:2, why:`Principle. The neighbor holds that Ines owes the money. A rule that an agreed payment is due once the work is done, needed or not, supports that directly. The first principle supports Ines. Helping without pay undercuts the neighbor too, and age and weather aren't the question.`, task:`LR12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Some historians claim that the founders of Linwood chose its site on the Corva River for the river trade. That can't be the whole story, since the river at Linwood was too shallow for loaded barges until a dam was built downstream forty years after the founding. Trade surely mattered, though: the founders' letters mention the road to the river crossing again and again. The likeliest explanation is that the site was chosen for the crossing, which carried the overland trade, rather than for the river itself.<br><br>The claim that the river was too shallow for loaded barges plays which one of the following roles in the argument?`,
      opts:[`It is the main conclusion the argument as a whole is meant to establish.`,`It is offered as evidence that trade had no part in choosing the site.`,`It is the claim the argument attributes to historians in order to reject it.`,`It is evidence offered against one explanation of why the site was chosen.`,`It explains why the founders' letters mention the river crossing so often.`],
      a:3, why:`Role of a statement. The shallow river is evidence against the historians' river trade explanation, which the argument says can't be the whole story. It isn't the conclusion. The argument says trade surely mattered, the historians' claim is about the river trade, and nothing ties the shallow water to the letters.`, task:`LR02`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the opinion section of the report?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`The victim died in the hospital after doctors treated a head injury he received that night.`,`The injury at the back of the skull caused the death, and the cut above the eyebrow did not.`,`The victim was struck twice, once from the front and once from behind, by the same person.`,`The fingernail scrapings collected from both hands will show who caused the fatal injury.`,`The cut above the eyebrow was a minor injury that would have healed within a few days.`],
      a:1, why:`Main idea. The opinion section names the fracture at the back of the skull as the fatal injury and calls the brow laceration separate and non-contributory. The hospital detail comes from the first paragraph. Nowhere does it say who caused either injury, or whether one person caused both. It makes no guess about the scrapings, and it doesn't say how fast a cut like that heals.`, task:`RC01`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`As it is used in the report, the word "non-contributory" most nearly means`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`not a cause of the death`,`not caused by another person`,`not visible without close study`,`not treated at the hospital`,`not related to the night's events`],
      a:0, why:`Meaning in context. The word comes right after the fatal injury and the cause of death, and it's said of the cut. So it means the cut had no part in why he died. Who caused it, whether anybody could see it, whether the hospital treated it, and whether it came from that night are all things the report leaves alone.`, task:`RC07`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the organization of the excerpt?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`It states a conclusion about the death, then lists the injuries that led the pathologist to it.`,`It compares two possible causes of the death and gives the evidence on each side of the question.`,`It describes the events of the night in order, from the first injury through the time of death.`,`It reports what the hospital found, then corrects the hospital's account of the injuries.`,`It records findings from the outside of the body, then the inside, then gives an opinion on them.`],
      a:4, why:`Organization. The excerpt identifies the body, records the external findings and then the internal one, and ends with an opinion that ranks the injuries and states the cause of death. The opinion comes last, not first. It doesn't weigh two causes, narrate the night, or correct the hospital.`, task:`RC09`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Students taking multiple choice tests should change an answer whenever they feel any doubt about it.`,`Test preparation guides have given students poor advice about answer changing for most of a century.`,`Memory is generally too unreliable to serve as any kind of guide to how a person should take a test.`,`Changed answers mostly help, and the belief that they hurt lasts because the costly ones stick in memory.`,`A 2005 study was the first to show that changed answers are more often right than wrong on a test.`],
      a:3, why:`Main idea. The passage sets the common belief against the evidence and spends most of its length on why the belief survives: the costly switches sting, so they're what people remember. Changing at any doubt contradicts the last paragraph, the test guides are a detail, memory in general is too broad, and the passage says the research goes back to the 1920s.`, task:`RC01`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The primary purpose of the passage is to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`argue that multiple choice tests should be replaced with other kinds of tests`,`describe a method of changing answers that will reliably raise test scores`,`explain why a common belief survives evidence against it, and what the evidence shows`,`criticize the researchers who studied answer changing for concluding too much`,`compare the test taking habits of students in the 1920s with those of students today`],
      a:2, why:`Primary purpose. The passage presents the belief, the counting that contradicts it, the memory explanation, and the narrow lesson the author draws from it. Nothing proposes replacing tests or offers a reliable method, the author agrees with the researchers, and there's no comparison across generations.`, task:`RC02`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The author mentions changing lines at a grocery store primarily in order to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`suggest that test takers should behave as they do when they shop`,`give a familiar case in which memories picked by feeling mislead`,`show that people usually choose the slower line at the store`,`point out that the researchers also studied grocery shoppers`,`argue that waiting in line is more stressful than taking a test`],
      a:1, why:`Function of a detail. The grocery line is an everyday version of the same pattern: the switch that went wrong is remembered, the stay that went wrong fades, and the memory turns into a sample chosen by feeling. Nothing recommends shopping habits, ranks the lines, says shoppers were studied, or compares stress.`, task:`RC08`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    ((s.flags.crammed||G.crammed)
      ?`She'd slept two hours. The vents were going, and the engine under them, and a rattle somewhere in the dash that Teo would have fixed on any other day of the year, and she couldn't tell which of the sounds were the truck's and which were hers.`
      :`She was so tired her hands felt like they belonged to somebody in the back seat. Four hours of being careful with every word on a screen, and now this, in a parked truck that smelled like Bianca's coffee.`),
    `He still had the envelope. He turned it over to the front. He'd read it already, she could tell by how he didn't hurry. He read it again anyway, MAYBE HE DID IT across the top in her capitals and every line under it, slowly, like a parts manual. Then he set it on the dash with his own name up.`,
    ((s.flags.pushedteo||G.pushedteo)
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You think it was Pa."</span> He laughed once through his nose, the way their father laughed at the radio when the Rockies blew a lead in the eighth, and in a closed truck it was a terrible sound. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"How long have you had that in there? How long, Dani?"</span>`
      :`He asked her if she thought it was Pa. He asked it quietly, to the envelope and not to her, because that was what it said across the top in her own capitals. She'd been carrying it around in the back of her book since December.`),
    `She didn't answer him. All morning she'd picked whichever answer had to be true, five choices at a time, a hundred times or near it, and nobody had ever handed her a sheet with a line on it for this one.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"It wasn't Pa,"</span> he said.`,
    `Out past the tree a man in a polo shirt was walking a girl with a clear plastic bag to a minivan, his arm around her shoulders, and somebody honked twice at somebody in the next row, happy about something. Her vent was on high, aimed at her knees. She left it.`,
    ((s.flags.pushedteo||G.pushedteo)
      ?`He wasn't angry anymore. He said it to the windshield. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I hit him. The man. Dani, I'm the one that hit him."</span>`
      :`He said it to the windshield. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I hit him. The man. Dani, I'm the one that hit him."</span>`)
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch09.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Non-contributory`
});
